oil rigs are used to drill new wells and  when oil prices dropped last year they  started shutting down at an  unprecedented rate more than a third of  us rigs have been taken offline in just  three months it makes sense that if  Riggs lead to new wells then fewer rigs  should lead to less oil right not  necessarily so far oil production hasn't  taken a hit in fact it's still going up  according to US Energy forecast the US  will pump almost eight percent more oil  this year levels not seen since 1972 so  rig counts are down but oil production  is up why here are three main reasons  number one efficiency it's mostly older  less efficient rigs that are being taken  offline newer machines including  horizontal rigs used in fracking are  doing better and oil drillers are  focusing their remaining rigs on regions  that crank out the most oil squeezing  more oil from fewer rigs number 2 sunk  costs new wells can cost 12 million to  drill oil companies pay that fee upfront  and it takes years to make that money  back for the most part it doesn't make  financial sense to stop pumping from  wells that have already been drill so  the pumping continues number 3 oil  prices the price of crude has dropped by  about fifty percent since June but at  around fifty dollars a barrel many  shells are still profitable shale wells  crank out a lot of oil for about 18  months rigs only collapse in the last  few months so there's a lag there and  production hasn't caught it yet  in the meantime American drillers are  pumping oil so fast they're running out  of places to stash it storage tanks that  hold us oil are at the highest levels in  at least 80 years the slowdown may be  coming but it's not here yet
 

